{"h":3395,"w":2400,"lines":[{"t":"1718867188","c":0.30000001192092896,"x":0.77160493914987061,"w":0.069958845774332645,"h":0.008720930389296444,"y":0.046511627815782663},{"c":1,"t":"READING","h":0.017724969502191823,"y":0.094335189491720706,"w":0.12764897664388014,"x":0.42794505961966783},{"t":"READING PASSAGE 1","y":0.15400471134213145,"c":1,"w":0.28806584676106767,"x":0.10082304359551496,"h":0.013250883392226132},{"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 1-13, which are based on Reading","x":0.1028806523648108,"y":0.1889534882261894,"h":0.015993508068214979,"c":1,"w":0.76131688435872402},{"t":"Passage 1 below.","y":0.2049418603347376,"x":0.10493826878694217,"w":0.15637860616048177,"h":0.013081395583944722,"c":1},{"w":0.19135801951090492,"h":0.02193414982412667,"t":"The thylacine","x":0.39711934403192761,"y":0.23837209337444376,"c":1},{"x":0.10283721289124022,"y":0.27570222007330292,"c":1,"w":0.77170959472656242,"h":0.017579915526337309,"t":"The extinct thylacine, also known as the Tasmanian tiger, was a marsupial* that bore a superficial"},{"y":0.29337467279584195,"h":0.015484208914641084,"x":0.10078204056584393,"t":"resemblance to a dog. Its most distinguishing feature was the 13-19 dark brown stripes over its","c":1,"w":0.75729293823242183},{"y":0.30962905156226295,"x":0.10283008979989859,"w":0.76963495890299483,"t":"back, beginning at the rear of the body and extending onto the tail. The thylacine's average nose-","c":1,"h":0.016100785757543368},{"x":0.098765436701034923,"y":0.32703488357417876,"h":0.016015764400450716,"c":1,"w":0.63580245971679694,"t":"to-tail length for adult males was 162.6 cm, compared to 153.7 cm for females."},{"c":1,"x":0.10082304578557853,"t":"The thylacine appeared to occupy most types of terrain except dense rainforest, with open","h":0.014575971731448756,"w":0.71810699462890626,"y":0.36042402839693033},{"x":0.10082305782304625,"y":0.37632508815731036,"t":"eucalyptus forest thought to be its prime habitat. In terms of feeding, it was exclusively","w":0.69547322591145833,"c":1,"h":0.016195524146054185},{"h":0.013081395583944611,"t":"carnivorous, and its stomach was muscular with an ability to distend so that it could eat large","w":0.74074076334635419,"c":1,"y":0.39389534871734444,"x":0.10288064704615862},{"y":0.40988372078303414,"h":0.01598837238037687,"t":"amounts of food at one time, probably an adaptation to compensate for long periods when","w":0.71810699462890626,"x":0.10082304582245513,"c":1},{"t":"hunting was unsuccessful and food scarce. The thylacine was not a fast runner and probably","y":0.42726737344388643,"w":0.73045267740885422,"h":0.013250883392226132,"x":0.10288065707599618,"c":1},{"x":0.10082304108427943,"w":0.74897120157877606,"y":0.44316843320054211,"c":1,"t":"caught its prey by exhausting it during a long pursuit. During long-distance chases, thylacines","h":0.016195524146054074},{"w":0.75720163981119804,"h":0.014534883982160629,"y":0.46075581382074193,"c":1,"t":"were likely to have relied more on scent than any other sense. They emerged to hunt during the","x":0.10082304830880304},{"y":0.47673733831681142,"w":0.77983540852864586,"h":0.015995219964160978,"x":0.1008230363769953,"c":1,"t":"evening, night and early morning and tended to retreat to the hills and forest for shelter during the"},{"c":1,"t":"day. Despite the common name 'tiger, the thylacine had a shy, nervous temperament. Although","w":0.76131688435872402,"x":0.1008230391333952,"y":0.49263839807186516,"h":0.017667844522968101},{"c":1,"h":0.016032884483202614,"x":0.10082303732091323,"t":"mainly nocturnal, it was sighted moving during the day and some individuals were even recorded","y":0.51017441845873512,"w":0.77366256713867188},{"h":0.018425624137491936,"x":0.10048314219541159,"t":"basking in the sun.","y":0.5247468144114481,"w":0.15489458719889321,"c":1},{"x":0.10288065800972145,"y":0.55948174314668597,"h":0.014723203769140158,"t":"The thylacine had an extended breeding season from winter to spring, with indications that","w":0.72427983601888024,"c":1},{"w":0.74897120157877606,"c":1,"x":0.10082304125450767,"t":"some breeding took place throughout the year. The thylacine, like all marsupials, was tiny and","y":0.57558139518464513,"h":0.017469252798666846},{"c":1,"h":0.01453488398216074,"x":0.10082304736209892,"y":0.59302325568137415,"t":"hairless when born. Newborns crawled into the pouch on the belly of their mother, and attached","w":0.76337448120117191},{"t":"themselves to one of the four teats, remaining there for up to three months. When old enough to","x":0.10288065238266038,"c":1,"y":0.60895170804549148,"w":0.76131688435872402,"h":0.01472320376914027},{"t":"leave the pouch, the young stayed in a lair such as a deep rocky cave, well-hidden nest or hollow","y":0.62411900415599064,"h":0.019030208160795814,"c":1,"x":0.098694141058904186,"w":0.7696910095214845},{"y":0.64116190107231719,"c":1,"t":"log, whilst the mother hunted.","x":0.098588154961772284,"h":0.017527060177357168,"w":0.24513509114583334},{"c":1,"y":0.67579505306656362,"t":"Approximately 4,000 years ago, the thylacine was widespread throughout New Guinea and most","x":0.10082305457716624,"h":0.016195524146054185,"w":0.77160491943359377},{"h":0.016031172025610885,"w":0.76954732259114578,"x":0.10082304640164855,"t":"of mainland Australia, as well as the island of Tasmania. The most recent, well-dated occurrence","y":0.69186046496998976,"c":1},{"t":"of a thylacine on the mainland is a carbon-dated fossil from Murray Cave in Western Australia,","y":0.70930232544873295,"w":0.7592592366536457,"c":1,"x":0.10288066963498077,"h":0.01453488398216074},{"x":0.10288065519154657,"y":0.72526501741753802,"c":1,"w":0.74279836018880208,"h":0.014560564106288587,"t":"which is around 3,100 years old. Its extinction coincided closely with the arrival of wild dogs"},{"y":0.74116607776057897,"x":0.1028806689800434,"h":0.014723203769140158,"c":1,"t":"called dingoes in Australia and a similar predator in New Guinea. Dingoes never reached","w":0.70781890869140618},{"h":0.014541732127590579,"c":1,"t":"Tasmania, and most scientists see this as the main reason for the thylacine's survival there.","y":0.75872093009961361,"x":0.10288066711253435,"w":0.72016459147135414},{"y":0.83421672563640148,"w":0.74897120157877595,"h":0.010306242638398189,"c":1,"x":0.10082304107981571,"t":"* marsupial: a mammal, such as a kangaroo, whose young are born incompletely developed and are typically carried and suckled in a"},{"y":0.84738372081202695,"w":0.1604938252766927,"c":1,"h":0.010174418787512574,"t":"pouch on the mother's belly","x":0.106995885813029},{"c":1,"h":0.013081395583944722,"w":0.024691357612609766,"y":0.86773255795657411,"t":"59","x":0.85802469146118487},{"t":"IELTSXpress.com","x":0.66991655033568553,"w":0.30612678527832038,"c":1,"h":0.03375352650564889,"y":0.95314993796778535}]}